@@@ problem 17 letter-combinations-of-a-phone-number
title: Letter Combinations of a Phone Number
difficulty: Medium
topics: Hash Table, String, Backtracking
params: digits
time: O(4^n · n)
space: O(n) extra (output excluded)
stress: 4
bench: n=5..10 step=+1 time=4^n space=4^n unit=number_of_digits_(all_4-letter_keys)

=== description
Given a string of digits `2-9`, return all letter combinations the number could represent on a phone keypad, in any order (the judge sorts them).

The mapping is `2: abc, 3: def, 4: ghi, 5: jkl, 6: mno, 7: pqrs, 8: tuv, 9: wxyz`. An empty input has no combinations.

=== constraints
- `0 <= digits.length <= 4`
- `digits[i]` is in `'2'..'9'`

=== hints
- Build the combination one digit at a time; for each digit try each of its letters, recurse, then undo (backtrack).

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> letterCombinations(string digits) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::letterCombinations).sort_output();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        if (n <= 4) return lc::lines(g.str(g.irange(0, n), "23456789"));
        return lc::lines(g.str(n, "79"));   // benchmark: 4 letters per key
    });
}

=== approach
**Backtracking**: keep a partial string `cur`. At depth `i`, append each letter mapped to `digits[i]`, recurse to depth `i + 1`, then remove the letter. When `cur` has as many letters as there are digits, it's a complete combination.

There are up to `4^n` combinations, each of length `n`, so the output alone is O(4^n · n). The recursion itself needs only O(n) extra space.

=== solution
class Solution {
    const vector<string> keys = {"", "", "abc", "def", "ghi", "jkl", "mno", "pqrs", "tuv", "wxyz"};
    vector<string> out;
    string cur;

    void build(const string &digits, size_t i) {
        if (i == digits.size()) {            // one full combination
            out.push_back(cur);
            return;
        }
        for (char c : keys[digits[i] - '0']) {
            cur.push_back(c);                // choose
            build(digits, i + 1);            // explore
            cur.pop_back();                  // un-choose
        }
    }

public:
    vector<string> letterCombinations(string digits) {
        out.clear();
        cur.clear();
        if (digits.empty()) return out;
        build(digits, 0);
        return out;
    }
};

=== examples
"23"
> ["ad","ae","af","bd","be","bf","cd","ce","cf"]

""
> []

"2"
> ["a","b","c"]

