@@@ problem 46 permutations
title: Permutations
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Backtracking
params: nums
time: O(n · n!)
space: O(n) extra (output excluded)
stress: 6
bench: n=3..9 step=+1 time=n! space=n! unit=nums.length

=== description
Given an array `nums` of **distinct** integers, return all possible permutations, in any order (the judge sorts them).

=== constraints
- `1 <= nums.length <= 6`
- `-10 <= nums[i] <= 10`, all distinct

=== hints
- Build a permutation position by position, choosing among the unused numbers.
- Or permute in place: swap each candidate into position `i`, recurse on `i + 1`, swap back.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> permute(vector<int>& nums) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::permute).sort_output();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) { return lc::lines(g.distinct(std::max(n, 1), -10, 10)); });
}

=== approach
**In-place backtracking with swaps.** For position `i`, every element in `nums[i..]` can be placed there: swap it into position `i`, recurse on `i + 1`, then swap back to restore the array. When `i` reaches the end, `nums` holds a complete permutation.

There are `n!` permutations of length `n`, so producing them costs O(n · n!).

=== solution
class Solution {
    vector<vector<int>> out;

    void permuteFrom(vector<int> &nums, size_t i) {
        if (i == nums.size()) { out.push_back(nums); return; }
        for (size_t j = i; j < nums.size(); ++j) {
            swap(nums[i], nums[j]);        // put nums[j] at position i
            permuteFrom(nums, i + 1);
            swap(nums[i], nums[j]);        // undo
        }
    }

public:
    vector<vector<int>> permute(vector<int>& nums) {
        out.clear();
        permuteFrom(nums, 0);
        return out;
    }
};

=== examples
