> [[-1,5],[5,-1]]

[4,-3,0]
> [[-3,0,4],[-3,4,0],[0,-3,4],[0,4,-3],[4,-3,0],[4,0,-3]]

@@@ problem 39 combination-sum
title: Combination Sum
difficulty: Medium
topics: Array, Backtracking
params: candidates, target
time: exponential in target / min(candidates)
space: O(target / min(candidates))
stress: 20
bench: off

=== description
Given an array of **distinct** positive integers `candidates` and a `target`, return all **unique** combinations of candidates that sum to `target`. The same number may be used an **unlimited** number of times. Two combinations are the same if they use each number the same number of times.

Any order is accepted (the judge sorts the combinations and their contents).

=== constraints
- `1 <= candidates.length <= 30`
- `2 <= candidates[i] <= 40`, all distinct
- `1 <= target <= 40`

=== hints
- To avoid duplicates like `[2,3]` and `[3,2]`, only allow choosing candidates at index `>= ` the last chosen index.
- Sort the candidates so you can stop as soon as a candidate exceeds the remaining target.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int>> combinationSum(vector<int>& candidates, int target) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::combinationSum).sort_deep();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) {
        return lc::lines(g.distinct(g.irange(1, std::min(8, std::max(1, n))), 2, 40), g.irange(1, std::min(40, 8 + n)));
    });
}

=== approach
Sort the candidates and backtrack with a `start` index: from position `i`, the next number may be `candidates[i]` again (unlimited reuse) or any later candidate, but never an earlier one. That makes every multiset appear in exactly one (non-decreasing) order, so there are no duplicates. Because the array is sorted, once a candidate exceeds the remaining target, all later ones do too, so we stop the loop.

=== solution
class Solution {
    vector<vector<int>> out;
    vector<int> cur;

    void search(const vector<int> &c, size_t start, int remaining) {
        if (remaining == 0) { out.push_back(cur); return; }
        for (size_t i = start; i < c.size() && c[i] <= remaining; ++i) {
            cur.push_back(c[i]);
            search(c, i, remaining - c[i]);   // i, not i+1: reuse allowed
            cur.pop_back();
        }
    }

public:
    vector<vector<int>> combinationSum(vector<int>& candidates, int target) {
        sort(candidates.begin(), candidates.end());
        out.clear();
        cur.clear();
        search(candidates, 0, target);
        return out;
    }
};

