@@@ problem 52 n-queens-ii
title: N-Queens II
difficulty: Hard
topics: Backtracking
params: n
time: O(n!)
space: O(n)
stress: 9
bench: n=4..12 step=+1 time=4^n space=n unit=board_size

=== description
The **n-queens** puzzle asks how to place `n` queens on an `n x n` chessboard so that no two queens attack each other (no two share a row, column or diagonal).

Given `n`, return the **number** of distinct solutions.

=== constraints
- `1 <= n <= 9`

=== hints
- Place one queen per row. For each row, try every column that is not attacked.
- Track attacked columns and both diagonals: cells on the same `\` diagonal share `row - col`, cells on the same `/` diagonal share `row + col`.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    int totalNQueens(int n) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::totalNQueens);
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) { return lc::lines(std::max(1, std::min(n, 12))); });
}

=== approach
Backtrack **row by row**: every row gets exactly one queen, so we only choose its column. A column is allowed if no earlier queen uses the same column, the same `row - col` diagonal, or the same `row + col` anti-diagonal. Keep three sets as bit masks so each check is O(1); place, recurse to the next row, then remove the queen. Reaching row `n` means one complete solution.

=== solution
class Solution {
    int n = 0, count = 0;

    // cols / diag / anti: bit masks of attacked columns and diagonals
    void place(int row, unsigned cols, unsigned long long diag, unsigned long long anti) {
        if (row == n) { ++count; return; }
        for (int c = 0; c < n; ++c) {
            unsigned long long d = 1ULL << (row - c + n), a = 1ULL << (row + c);
            if ((cols >> c & 1) || (diag & d) || (anti & a)) continue;   // attacked
            place(row + 1, cols | (1u << c), diag | d, anti | a);
        }
    }

public:
    int totalNQueens(int n) {
        this->n = n;
        count = 0;
        place(0, 0, 0, 0);
        return count;
    }
};

=== examples
4
> 2
: There are two ways to place 4 queens on a 4x4 board.

1
> 1

=== tests
2
> 0

3
> 0

5
> 10

8
> 92

9
> 352

@@@ problem 22 generate-parentheses
title: Generate Parentheses
difficulty: Medium
topics: String, Dynamic Programming, Backtracking
params: n
time: O(4^n / √n)
space: O(n) extra (output excluded)
stress: 8
bench: n=4..12 step=+1 time=4^n space=4^n unit=pairs_of_parentheses

=== description
Given `n` pairs of parentheses, return all combinations of **well-formed** parentheses, in any order (the judge sorts them).

=== constraints
- `1 <= n <= 8`

=== hints
- Add `'('` while you still have some left; add `')'` only if it wouldn't close more than you opened.

=== template
class Solution {
public:
    vector<string> generateParenthesis(int n) {

    }
};

=== driver
LC_DRIVER {
    h.solve(&Solution::generateParenthesis).sort_output();
    h.gen([](lc::Gen &g, int n) { return lc::lines(std::max(1, std::min(n, 12))); });
}

=== approach
Backtrack while tracking how many `(` and `)` have been used. You may add `(` if fewer than `n` have been used, and `)` only if fewer `)` than `(` have been used, so the prefix never becomes invalid. Every leaf of this search is a valid string, so no work is wasted. The number of results is the Catalan number C(n) ≈ 4^n / (n^1.5 √π).

=== solution
class Solution {
    vector<string> out;
    string cur;

    void build(int open, int close, int n) {
        if ((int)cur.size() == 2 * n) { out.push_back(cur); return; }
        if (open < n) {                  // can still open
            cur.push_back('(');
            build(open + 1, close, n);
            cur.pop_back();
        }
        if (close < open) {              // can close without going negative
            cur.push_back(')');
            build(open, close + 1, n);
            cur.pop_back();
        }
    }

public:
    vector<string> generateParenthesis(int n) {
        out.clear();
        cur.clear();
        build(0, 0, n);
        return out;
    }
};

=== examples
